# Vendor properties for Motorola SM8635 (arcfox), ported from the shipping
# firmware's /vendor/build.prop (W1UXS36H).
#
# THIS FILE WAS EMPTY, AND THAT WAS THE BOOT BLOCKER.
#
# Our built vendor/build.prop had 81 lines against stock's 487 -- 393 device
# properties simply did not exist. The one that stopped the boot:
#
#   vendor.gatekeeper.is_security_level_spu=0
#
# libqtikeymint.so imports android::base::WaitForPropertyCreation and calls it
# from AndroidKeyMintDevice's constructor -- i.e. BEFORE addService -- with an
# infinite timeout. With the property absent, keymint-qti started, waited
# forever for it to be *created*, and never registered IKeyMintDevice/default.
# keystore2 then failed its six retries and aborted, and init never left
# late-fs. The /proc forensics show it precisely: futex op 0x0 (FUTEX_WAIT
# WITHOUT the private flag, which no pthread primitive uses) on uaddr
# page_base+4 -- that address is prop_area::serial_, i.e. __system_property_wait
# on a property that does not exist.
#
# The same missing property is why android.hardware.gatekeeper-service-qti
# never started: its .rc is `disabled` and enabled only by
#   on property:vendor.gatekeeper.is_security_level_spu=0
#
# Build-identity keys (ro.build.*, ro.product.*, ro.vendor.build.*, ro.odm.*,
# ro.system.*, ro.treble/vndk/api_level, ro.board.*) are deliberately EXCLUDED --
# the build system generates those and duplicating stock's would misreport the
# ROM. Everything else stock's vendor sets is here.
#
# Regenerate with the method in HANDOFF-NEXT.md 0.5g.


