# --- USB: without these the device never enumerates, so there is no adb --------
# init.mmi.usb.sh is unlabelled (u:object_r:vendor_file:s0), so init refuses it:
#   Could not start service 'vendor.mmi-usb-sh' ... has incorrect label or no
#   domain transition from u:r:init:s0
# That script is what restores persist.sys.usb.config after init.mmi.usb.rc
# deliberately blanks it at load_bpf_programs. Without it:
#   Command 'setprop vendor.usb.config ${sys.usb.config}' ... failed:
#   property 'sys.usb.config' doesn't exist
# and since vendor.prop sets vendor.usb.use_gadget_hal=0, Motorola's design has
# the INIT RC bind the UDC, not the HAL -- every
#   write /config/usb_gadget/g1/UDC ${sys.usb.controller}
# in init.mmi.usb.rc is gated on sys.usb.config and sys.usb.configfs, neither of
# which is ever set. So the UDC is never bound, and the result is
#   libusbconfigfs: Gadget cannot be pulled down
#   android.hardware.usb.gadget-service.qti: Usb Gadget setcurrent functions failed
#   UsbDeviceManager: Usb setCurrentUsbFunctionsCb failed, functions:1, status:1
# while adbd sits waiting on a gadget that never pulls up. dwc3 itself is healthy
# (b_sess_vld, turn on gadget, role DEVICE) and there is not one USB or configfs
# SELinux denial in the whole capture.
#
# persist.vendor.usb.config is Motorola's own entry point: init.mmi.usb.rc has
# `on property:persist.vendor.usb.config=*` which sets persist.sys.usb.config.
# Labelling init.mmi.usb.sh properly is still the complete fix (it needs stock's
# init_usb domain plus the vendor_mot_*_prop types we do not have yet).
sys.usb.configfs=1
sys.usb.controller=a600000.dwc3
persist.vendor.usb.config=adb
